PROXY-CONTROL IDENTIFIABILITY UNDER INTERVENTION-INDUCED MEASUREMENT DISTORTION

A Theoretical Framework for Latent-State Identification When Interventions Modify Both Biological Dynamics and Their Observational Proxies

Ouadi Maakoul + AI Collaborative Research
Theoretical Research Draft — Gate 1
Version 0.1

---

Abstract

Many control problems operate on latent states that cannot be observed directly. The controller therefore relies on measurable proxy variables. This becomes fundamentally more difficult when the intervention used to modify the latent state also directly modifies the proxy itself. In such systems, an observed improvement in the proxy may result either from a genuine change in the latent state or from an intervention-induced measurement effect.

This work formalizes that problem as Proxy-Control Identifiability (PCI).

We consider partially observed dynamical systems of the form

[
X_{t+1}=F_\theta(X_t,U_t)+\eta_t,
]

[
Y_t=h_\phi(X_t,U_t)+\epsilon_t,
]

where X_t is a latent state, Y_t is an observable proxy, and U_t is an intervention that may affect both the latent dynamics and the observation mechanism. We define an observational-interventional equivalence relation over candidate models and introduce the Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap

[
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}},
]

which measures the maximum disagreement between latent-target distributions among models that are observationally indistinguishable under the available intervention regime.

We first construct an analytical counterexample demonstrating that identical intervention-conditioned proxy distributions can coexist with different latent-state trajectories. Consequently,

[
M_i\sim_{\mathcal D,U}M_j
\not\Rightarrow
M_i\sim_T M_j.
]

We then formalize a geometric criterion for diagnostic interventions and show, through a pathological counterexample, that eliminating the direct intervention-to-proxy channel is not sufficient for latent-state identification. Diagnostic excitation must also propagate through the system dynamics into the latent modes relevant to the target functional.

The resulting framework distinguishes three logically separate requirements:

1. proxy-channel separation,
2. dynamic excitation, and
3. target-relevant observability.

The central research conjecture is that contraction of \Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} requires a joint observability-excitation condition rather than merely a reduction of direct proxy distortion. The work therefore reframes the problem from the construction of another POMDP/CBF architecture into a more fundamental question in nonlinear system identification and causal inference:

[
\boxed{
\text{When can an intervention-sensitive proxy identify a latent causal target?}
}
]

The framework is intended as a theoretical foundation for applications in which the measured proxy may respond substantially faster than the latent state itself, including biological aging and intervention-sensitive aging biomarkers.

