1. Introduction

1.1 The latent-state control problem

A large class of scientific and engineering systems cannot be controlled using direct measurements of the state of interest.

Let

[
X_t\in\mathcal X\subseteq\mathbb R^n
]

denote a latent state and

[
Y_t\in\mathcal Y\subseteq\mathbb R^m
]

the observable measurements available to an agent.

The system evolves according to

[
X_{t+1}=F_\theta(X_t,U_t)+\eta_t,
\tag{1}
]

while measurements satisfy

[
Y_t=h_\phi(X_t,U_t)+\epsilon_t.
\tag{2}
]

Here:

- X_t is the latent state;
- Y_t is the observable proxy;
- U_t is the intervention;
- F_\theta is the latent-state transition operator;
- h_\phi is the observation operator;
- \eta_t represents process uncertainty;
- \epsilon_t represents measurement uncertainty.

The critical feature of Eq. (2) is the explicit dependence

[
h_\phi=h_\phi(X_t,U_t).
]

Thus the intervention does not merely change the underlying system. It may also change the measurement channel.

The causal structure is therefore

[
U_t\longrightarrow X_{t+1}\longrightarrow Y_{t+1}
]

together with a second pathway

[
U_t\longrightarrow Y_t.
]

The latter pathway creates the central difficulty of this work.

---

1.2 Proxy improvement is not necessarily latent-state improvement

Suppose a controller seeks to minimize a target functional

[
T:\mathcal X\rightarrow\mathbb R_{\geq0},
]

where, for example,

[
T(X)=D_X(X,\mathcal X_Y)
]

measures distance from a target region \mathcal X_Y.

A naive controller may instead optimize a proxy functional

[
S:\mathcal Y\rightarrow\mathbb R.
]

It may therefore interpret

[
\Delta S>0
]

as evidence that

[
\Delta T<0.
]

This inference is not generally valid.

The intervention may produce

[
U\rightarrow Y
]

without producing the desired

[
U\rightarrow X\rightarrow Y
]

effect.

The fundamental problem is consequently not merely partial observability.

It is partial observability combined with intervention-dependent measurement.

---

2. Scope and Relation to Prior Control Architectures

The present framework does not claim novelty from combining:

- POMDPs,
- dual control,
- belief-space control,
- control barrier functions,
- active learning,
- uncertainty-aware control.

These are established areas of research.

The theoretical object introduced here is instead the identifiability relation between:

[
\text{observable intervention-conditioned behavior}
]

and

[
\text{latent target behavior}.
]

The central question is therefore not

[
\text{How should we control a partially observed system?}
]

but

[
\boxed{
\text{When does the observable response identify the latent causal response?}
}
]

This distinction is essential.

