6. Definition 1: Intervention-Conditioned Observational Equivalence

Two models

[
M_i,M_j\in\mathcal M
]

are said to be intervention-conditioned observationally equivalent under (\mathcal D_t,U_{t:T}), written

[
M_i\sim_{\mathcal D,U}M_j,
]

if

[
P_{M_i}(Y_{t:T}\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t)

P_{M_j}(Y_{t:T}\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t)
]

as probability measures.

Equivalently, whenever the relevant densities exist,

[
D_{\mathrm{KL}}
\left(
P_{M_i}(Y_{t:T}\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t)
\Vert
P_{M_j}(Y_{t:T}\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t)
\right)
=0.
\tag{8}
]

The equality of distributions is the fundamental definition; the KL representation is only a convenient equivalent characterization under appropriate measure-theoretic conditions.

---

7. Definition 2: Latent-Target Equivalence

Let

[
T:\mathcal X\rightarrow\mathbb R^q
]

be the target functional.

Two models are latent-target equivalent, written

[
M_i\sim_T M_j,
]

if

[
P_{M_i}
\left(
T(X_T)\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t
\right)

P_{M_j}
\left(
T(X_T)\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t
\right).
\tag{9}
]

The central theoretical question is therefore whether

[
\boxed{
M_i\sim_{\mathcal D,U}M_j
\Longrightarrow
M_i\sim_T M_j.
}
\tag{10}
]

If Eq. (10) holds over an admissible model class, the proxy is sufficient for identifying the target under the specified intervention regime.

If it does not hold, the proxy leaves unresolved latent causal ambiguity.

---

8. Definition 3: Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap

Define the Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap over horizon T as

[
\boxed{
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}
(\mathcal D_t,U_{t:T})

\sup_{\substack{
M_i,M_j\in\mathcal Q(\mathcal D_t,U)\
M_i\sim_{\mathcal D,U}M_j
}}
W_1
\left(
P_{M_i}(T(X_T)\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t),
P_{M_j}(T(X_T)\mid U_{t:T},\mathcal D_t)
\right).
}
\tag{11}
]

Here W_1 is the Wasserstein-1 distance.

This quantity has a direct interpretation:

[
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}=0
]

means that observationally indistinguishable models agree on the target distribution.

Conversely,

[
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}>0
]

means that at least two observationally indistinguishable models make different predictions about the latent target.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}=0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{target identification within }\mathcal Q.
}
]

The converse is also immediate under the definition when the supremum is attained or interpreted appropriately:

[
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}>0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{non-identifiability}.
]

For unbounded model classes, \Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} may be infinite. Therefore finite-gap claims require appropriate compactness or moment assumptions.

