9. The Direct Proxy-Distortion Decomposition

Assume differentiability of h_\phi.

For a small intervention perturbation

[
\Delta U_t
]

and corresponding latent change

[
\Delta X_t,
]

a first-order expansion gives

[
\Delta Y_t
\approx
D_Xh_\phi(X_t,U_t)\Delta X_t
+
D_Uh_\phi(X_t,U_t)\Delta U_t
+
\epsilon_t.
\tag{12}
]

Define

[
\Delta Y_t^{\mathrm{state}}

D_Xh_\phi\Delta X_t
]

and

[
\Delta Y_t^{\mathrm{direct}}

D_Uh_\phi\Delta U_t.
]

Then

[
\boxed{
\Delta Y_t
\approx
\Delta Y_t^{\mathrm{state}}
+
\Delta Y_t^{\mathrm{direct}}
+
\epsilon_t.
}
\tag{13}
]

Therefore, an observed change

[
\Delta Y_t>0
]

does not identify the sign or magnitude of

[
\Delta X_t.
]

