10. Multi-Time-Scale Structure

A particularly important regime is

[
\tau_Y\ll\tau_X,
\tag{14}
]

where:

- \tau_Y is the characteristic response time of the observable proxy;
- \tau_X is the characteristic response time of the latent state.

Define

[
\varepsilon_\tau

\frac{\tau_Y}{\tau_X}.
]

The regime of interest is

[
\varepsilon_\tau\ll1.
]

The distinction does not imply that all biological biomarkers satisfy this condition. Rather, it defines a mathematically meaningful regime in which direct measurement effects can occur on a faster timescale than genuine latent-state adaptation.

This motivates the asymptotic representation

[
\Delta Y

D_Xh,\Delta X
+
D_Uh,\Delta U
+
O(\varepsilon_\tau^2)
+
\epsilon.
\tag{15}
]

The theoretical problem is to determine when the second term can be separated from the first sufficiently well to identify the latent target.

