12. Interpretation of Theorem A

The theorem establishes an important impossibility result.

The observation distribution alone does not determine whether the observed response was produced by

[
U\rightarrow Y
]

or by

[
U\rightarrow X\rightarrow Y.
]

The two causal explanations can be observationally identical.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{proxy response}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{latent response}.
}
]

This result does not require an adversarial controller.

It is a structural property of the observation model.

---

13. Important Limitation of the Unbounded-Gap Claim

The preceding construction also clarifies an important correction.

It is not sufficient to claim that

[
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}\rightarrow\infty
]

merely because a controller exploits \beta U.

For a fixed admissible model class and bounded intervention set,

[
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}
]

may remain finite.

Unbounded divergence requires an additional assumption such as

[
\sup_{M\in\mathcal M}|\alpha|=\infty
]

or an unbounded intervention domain.

The rigorous claim is therefore:

[
\boxed{
D_Uh\neq0
\text{ permits arbitrarily large latent ambiguity only when the admissible model/intervention class permits it.}
}
]

This distinction is necessary for a valid theorem.

---

14. Diagnostic Interventions

The preceding theorem motivates interventions designed not only to alter the system but also to distinguish competing causal explanations.

Write

[
U_t

U_t^{\mathrm{ctrl}}
+
\delta U_t^{\mathrm{diag}}.
\tag{20}
]

A diagnostic perturbation should ideally satisfy

[
|D_UF,v|
\ll
|D_Uh,v|,
\tag{21}
]

meaning that the perturbation strongly affects the observation channel while minimally changing the latent dynamics.

However, such a direction need not exist.

Therefore diagnostic decoupling must be treated as an existence condition, not as an assumption.

