23. Corrected Theorem B: Conditional Identifiability Recovery

The original formulation of Theorem B is too strong if it claims convergence from the local rank condition alone.

A rigorous version requires additional assumptions.

Theorem B — Conditional Proxy-Control Identifiability Recovery

Consider a model class \mathcal Q satisfying:

A1. Regularity

F_\theta and h_\phi are sufficiently smooth on a compact admissible region.

A2. Bounded uncertainty

The process and observation noise satisfy finite moment conditions, and the admissible model class is sufficiently compact or otherwise controlled so that the relevant Wasserstein distances are finite.

A3. Diagnostic channel separation

There exists an admissible diagnostic direction v^\star such that

[
\rho(v^\star)\ge\rho_{\mathrm{crit}}>0.
]

A4. Persistent diagnostic excitation

The sequence

[
\delta U_t v^\star
]

is persistently exciting over the relevant horizon.

A5. Target-relevant observability

The diagnostic experiment induces a finite-horizon observation operator whose null space contains no direction that changes the target:

[
\delta X\in\ker\mathscr O_K(v^\star)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
dT(X)\delta X=0.
\tag{32}
]

A6. Statistical consistency

The estimator used to update the admissible model set is consistent under the specified experimental design.

Then, as the amount of informative diagnostic data increases,

[
\boxed{
\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}
\left(
\mathcal D_t\cup\mathcal D_{\mathrm{diag}}^N,
U\cup U_{\mathrm{diag}}^N
\right)
\longrightarrow0
}
]

provided the true data-generating system belongs to the admissible model class and the identifiability assumptions hold uniformly over that class.

---

Interpretation

The theorem does not claim that a large \rho is sufficient.

Instead:

[
\boxed{
\text{channel separation}
+
\text{persistent excitation}
+
\text{target-relevant observability}
+
\text{consistent inference}
}
]

is the structural package required for asymptotic contraction.

The result therefore separates two problems that are often conflated:

[
\text{estimating the direct proxy effect}
]

and

[
\text{identifying the latent target}.
