such that

[
P_{M_i}(Y_{0:T}\mid U)

P_{M_j}(Y_{0:T}\mid U)
]

but

[
P_{M_i}(T(X_T)\mid U)
\neq
P_{M_j}(T(X_T)\mid U).
]

Then no estimator based solely on

[
(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T})
]

can consistently distinguish the two target distributions.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{observational equivalence + target disagreement}
\Rightarrow
\text{fundamental non-identifiability}.
}
]

This is stronger than saying that a particular estimator fails.

It says that no estimator can succeed within the information structure.

