36. Safety Consequence

If a controller operates under latent-state uncertainty, safety constraints cannot generally be based only on

[
E[h_{\mathrm{safe}}(X_t)]\ge0.
]

Mean safety does not imply probabilistic safety.

A probabilistic requirement is instead

[
P(X_t\in\mathcal C_{\mathrm{safe}})
\ge1-\delta.
\tag{34}
]

Under a Gaussian approximation,

[
X_t\sim
\mathcal N(\hat X_t,\Sigma_t),
]

one may construct a confidence ellipsoid

[
\mathcal K_\delta

\left{
x:
(x-\hat X_t)^T
\Sigma_t^{-1}
(x-\hat X_t)
\le
\chi^2_n(1-\delta)
\right}.
]

A sufficient robust condition is then

[
h_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)\ge0
\qquad
\forall x\in\mathcal K_\delta.
]

For nonlinear h_{\mathrm{safe}}, local approximations may produce conservative conditions such as

[
h_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat X_t)

\sqrt{
\chi^2_n(1-\delta)
\nabla h_{\mathrm{safe}}^T
\Sigma_t
\nabla h_{\mathrm{safe}}
}
\ge0,
\tag{35}
]

but Eq. (35) is an approximation/sufficient local condition, not a universal probabilistic safety theorem.

This distinction prevents the safety layer from contaminating the identifiability theorem with an unjustified Gaussian assumption.

