41. Candidate Final Theoretical Contribution

The intended contribution can therefore be stated conservatively as follows:

«We formulate Proxy-Control Identifiability as the problem of identifying a latent causal target when interventions simultaneously modify the latent dynamics and the observation mechanism. We define an intervention-conditioned observational equivalence relation and a Wasserstein Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap measuring latent-target disagreement among observationally indistinguishable models. We prove constructively that direct intervention-sensitive observations can produce fundamental latent non-identifiability. We further show through counterexample that eliminating the direct intervention-to-proxy channel is insufficient: target-relevant dynamic excitation and observability are additionally required. We formulate a conditional identifiability-recovery result under explicit regularity, persistent-excitation, target-observability, and statistical-consistency assumptions.»

This is considerably stronger and safer than claiming:

«"We invented diagnostic interventions."»

The latter is almost certainly too broad.

The former identifies a specific mathematical problem.

